// Social Realism: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== social-realism.f01 | feature | chapters
title: Ninety Days
genre: social-realism + family
tone: Damp, crowded and humane
era: 1972 | period
setting: A disused air-force camp in the English countryside | fictional | institution
logline: A family of shopkeepers expelled from East Africa at ninety days' notice, with fifty pounds and one suitcase each, spends an autumn in the huts of an abandoned English airbase, waiting for a council somewhere to agree to take them.
premise: The decree gave every family of their origin ninety days to leave the country where they were born. They hold the passports of a third country that does not want them either and has admitted them with visible reluctance. A resettlement board runs sixteen camps. Some towns have taken out newspaper advertisements asking them not to come. {ba}, who owned two shops and a house with a mango tree, is given a camp bed and a blanket stamped with a crown.
synopsis: The film is in chapters named for the camp's own notices: Arrival, Canteen, Clothing Store, Resettlement Office, Departure. Arrival: a coach in the dark, volunteers with tea, coats donated by the Women's Institute, the first cold any of the children have felt. The grandmother {motiba} will not eat the canteen's food, which is boiled and contains beef. Canteen: within a week the women have negotiated a corner of the kitchen; within three there is a queue of English staff for the dal. {jayu}, the daughter, sixteen, becomes the hut's interpreter and so learns everything: who is hiding gold in a hem, whose husband was taken at a roadblock, which towns are on the red list. Clothing Store: {ba} is offered work at an hour's distance, sweeping a factory; he was an employer of twelve. He takes it and says nothing. A volunteer, {mrs_pike}, a brisk farmer's wife, teaches the grandmother to use a paraffin heater and is taught in return to make chapatis; neither has a word of the other's language and the scene is the film's happiest. Resettlement Office: the wait. The board's officer, {mr_ames}, an overworked civil servant with a map and coloured pins, has offers of housing in places where there is no work and work in places that have declared themselves full. The family is offered a council house in a Scottish new town where they will be the only family of their kind, or a wait of unknown length for a city where a cousin has a sofa. {ba} wants the cousin and the community. {jayu} wants the house and a school. A hut is daubed with paint one night; in the morning the local vicar and the village football team are scrubbing it off, embarrassed. Departure: the choice is made by {motiba}, who has not spoken on the subject, on the grounds that she has now moved countries three times in her life and the secret is to unpack. They go north. The last scene is a hardware shop two years later, with {ba}'s name over the door and {mrs_pike}, down for a visit, buying something she does not need.
question: What does a country owe the people it gave its passport to?
themes: dignity in transit; hospitality and its limits; the work of starting again, done mostly by women
audience: Adults and families with older children
viewpoint: With {jayu}, who translates for everyone and so sees every side
stakes: Where a family will live, what the father will be, and whether a girl's schooling survives
conflict: An expelled family against cold, bureaucracy, a divided welcome, and its own disagreement about where to begin
ending: The grandmother decides. They take the house in the north. Two years later there is a shop.
experience: Wet wool, steamed-up windows, comedy of mutual incomprehension, and a hard-earned small glow
look: Nissen huts in drizzle, saris under duffel coats, a map with coloured pins, a canteen hatch
sound: Rain on corrugated iron, three languages in one room, a tannoy mispronouncing names
m: belong | system | belonging-vs-identity | bittersweet
@ jayu | Jayshree | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 16 | A school, a desk, an exam | She was top of her class in a school that no longer admits her | Her father wants the family near its own | Sharp, impatient, funny, old before her time | From interpreter to the one whose future decides it | Three languages, switching mid-sentence | School blazer from another continent, a donated anorak
@ ba | Bapuji, Mr. Chandarana | co-lead | foil | human | he/him | 48 | To be a man with a shop again | He employed twelve people | He is sweeping a floor | Proud, gentle, silent about what he saw | From proprietor to labourer to proprietor | Formal, apologetic English; warm at home | A good suit worn daily, a ledger with nothing in it
@ motiba | Motiba | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 72 | Her family fed properly | She has done this before, twice | The cold, and beef in the gravy | Imperious, practical, secretly amused | From refusing the canteen to running a corner of it, to deciding | Commands in her own language only | White sari, a cardigan over it, a tin of spices
@ mrs_pike | Mrs. Pike | supporting | ally | human | she/her | 55 | To be of use | Her son emigrated and she knows what a welcome is worth | She has never met anyone from anywhere | Brisk, bossy, shy about kindness | From volunteer to friend | Country vowels, loud for foreigners | Headscarf, wellingtons, a thermos
@ mr_ames | Mr. Ames | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 39 | Everyone housed by Christmas | He believes in the job | The map has red areas | Harassed, fair, constrained | From pins to a handshake | Civil-service careful | Raincoat, a clipboard, coloured pins
~ jayu - ba | daughter and father | she speaks for him | he signs for the house she wanted
~ motiba - mrs_pike | two matriarchs | a paraffin heater | a chapati, and a visit two years on
~ ba - mr_ames | applicant and officer | a file | a choice explained honestly
# Arrival | EXT | Camp Gate | NIGHT | jayu, ba, motiba, mrs_pike | Get off the coach | Rain. Tea in thick cups. A pile of coats. A tannoy mangling their name. {motiba} looks at the hut and asks who is responsible | They arrive | 6 | crowd=120, vehicles=coach, vfx=rain
# Canteen | INT | Canteen | DAY | motiba, jayu, mrs_pike | Take a corner of the kitchen | Boiled beef and cabbage. The grandmother opens her tin. By the third week the cook is queueing at her end of the hatch | | 6 | crowd=100, props=spice tin
# Clothing Store | INT | Family Hut | NIGHT | ba, jayu | Take the job | Sweeping, an hour away by bus. He employed twelve. He irons his suit to go and do it. {jayu} translates the form and does not look at him | {ba} becomes a labourer | 5 | props=suit+form
# Clothing Store | INT | Family Hut | DAY | motiba, mrs_pike | Trade lessons | A paraffin heater, demonstrated loudly. A chapati, demonstrated silently. No shared word. A great deal of laughter | | 5 | props=paraffin heater+rolling pin
# Resettlement Office | INT | Resettlement Office | DAY | ba, jayu, mr_ames | Be offered | A map with pins. A house in the far north, now, the only family of their kind. Or wait, for a city marked red. {ba} says wait. {jayu} translates it, and then says what she thinks, in English | The family is divided | 7 | props=map with pins
# Resettlement Office | EXT | Hut Row | DAWN | jayu, mrs_pike | Scrub it off | Paint on a wall, two words. By morning a vicar and eleven footballers with buckets, not meeting anyone's eye | | 5 | crowd=20
# Departure | INT | Family Hut | NIGHT | motiba, ba, jayu | Decide | She has said nothing all week. She says she has moved countries three times and the secret is to unpack. She begins folding blankets | {motiba} chooses the house | 5 |
# Departure | INT | Hardware Shop, Two Years Later | DAY | ba, mrs_pike, jayu | Open | His name over the door. {jayu} doing homework behind the counter. {mrs_pike} buys a mop she does not need and counts her change twice out of politeness | | 4 | props=shop counter
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the resettlement camps for families expelled from Uganda in 1972, with invented people
